<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kavad II</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kavad II was a Sasanian king who deposed and killed his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> in 628 CE after the defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>. He made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> but died within the same year. His reign began a succession crisis in which many rulers claimed the throne within a few years, gravely weakening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kavad-ii.jpg" alt="Kavad II" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_the_Sasanian_king_Kavad_II_(cropped),_minted_at_Susa_in_628.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
